HPC-Applications

ASCOT5

ASCOT5 is a test-particle orbit-following code used to study particle motion,
transport and losses in magnetic-confinement fusion devices such as tokamaks
and stellarators.

This section documents the ASCOT5 installation and the data-driven HPC test
runner used in this project.

What the application calculates

A typical ASCOT5 simulation takes:

Magnetic field
      +
Plasma parameters
      +
Reactor / wall geometry
      +
Particle properties
      |
      v
   ASCOT5
      |
      v
Particle trajectories
Energy evolution
Collisions
Wall interactions
      |
      v
Diagnostics / distributions / performance data

A marker represents a simulated particle. Because many marker calculations can
be treated independently, ASCOT5 is well suited to MPI/OpenMP parallelism.

Why this application is useful for HPC

ASCOT5 uses multiple levels of parallelism:

MPI processes
      |
OpenMP threads
      |
SIMD / vector operations
      |
Particle calculations

The workload is primarily computational, but memory can also become important
for large magnetic fields, wall geometry, distributions, diagnostics and large
marker populations.

The ASCOT5 HDF5 design can contain multiple inputs and multiple simulation
results, with each item identified by a QID and optional description. This makes
it suitable for parameter scans and repeatable studies.

1. Requirements

The working environment used by this project includes:

Ubuntu 24.04

Python 3.12

Intel oneAPI compiler / MPI

HDF5

OpenMP

MPI

ASCOT5 / a5py

Install the basic Python tools:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-dev

Check:

python3 --version

Intel oneAPI

Load oneAPI before using the Intel compiler or Intel MPI:

source ~/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh

To load it automatically:

echo 'source ~/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh' >> ~/.profile

For Lmod/modulefile use, the existing project setup can also expose Intel
compiler, MPI and MKL modules.

HDF5

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y libhdf5-serial-dev hdf5-tools

Verify:

h5dump --version
h5cc -showconfig

The worker nodes need the HDF5 runtime libraries that the compiled ASCOT5
binary requires. Development headers are primarily needed where ASCOT5 is
compiled.

2. Install ASCOT5

Clone the source:

git clone https://github.com/ascot4fusion/ascot5.git

Create and activate the Python environment:

python3 -m venv ascot-env
source ascot-env/bin/activate

Install a5py and compile the main executable:

cd ascot5
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e .
make ascot5_main -j MPI=1
make libascot -j MPI=1

The executable used by this project is:

/home/ubuntu/ascot5/build/ascot5_main

Change that path in ascot_tests.json when your installation is somewhere
else.

3. Default text editor

ASCOT5 simulation options can be edited from the Ubuntu terminal with nano or
vim. To make nano the default editor:

echo 'export EDITOR=/usr/bin/nano' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

4. Jupyter visualisation

Install Jupyter:

pip install jupyter
ipython kernel install --user --name=ascotenv

Start the notebook server:

jupyter-notebook

Tutorials are stored in:

cd ~/ascot5/doc/tutorials

5. MPI networking and the headnode firewall

The Intel MPI process manager (Hydra) needs TCP connectivity between the
nodes. In this cluster, the headnode firewall uses an hn_table nftables
chain with a default drop policy. The relevant TCP chain allows SSH (22) and
NFS (2049) by default.

The MPI port range used by this project is:

40000-40100

The firewall must allow the MPI TCP/UDP traffic on the cluster interfaces.
For example, the working rules can include:

sudo nft add rule inet hn_table hn_tcp_chain tcp dport 40000-40100 accept
sudo nft add rule inet hn_table hn_udp_chain udp dport 40000-40100 accept

Inspect the active rules:

sudo nft list ruleset
sudo nft list chain inet hn_table hn_tcp_chain

Do not add these rules blindly to a different cluster. First inspect that
cluster's firewall policy and persistent nftables configuration.

Before an MPI run, the environment used by this project includes:

export I_MPI_HYDRA_IFACE=ens37
export I_MPI_PORT_RANGE=40000:40100
export HDF5_USE_FILE_LOCKING=FALSE

Test the network path with a simple TCP listener before debugging MPI itself.

6. Data-driven ASCOT5 tests

Why the script was changed

The original slowing-down script placed almost every parameter directly in
Python. That makes parameter scans difficult because changing the number of
markers, density, temperature or stopping condition requires editing code.

The new structure separates:

ascot_tests.json
      |
      |  test data / parameters
      v
run_ascot_tests.py
      |
      |  common simulation pipeline
      v
ASCOT5 + MPI
      |
      +---- runs/<test>/ascot.h5
      +---- plots
      +---- summaries
      +---- benchmark report

The JSON file is now the place where test data is added or changed.

Files

Keep these files together:

ascot_tests.json
run_ascot_tests.py

The important sections of ascot_tests.json are:

paths
  -> ASCOT executable and output root

mpi
  -> nodes, MPI ranks, OpenMP threads and Hydra networking

common
  -> shared field, plasma, AFSI, marker and simulation settings

tests
  -> individual test cases

Run available tests

List all configured tests:

python3 run_ascot_tests.py --list

Run the baseline:

python3 run_ascot_tests.py --test slowingdown_baseline

Run a different test:

python3 run_ascot_tests.py --test high_density

Run the complete suite:

python3 run_ascot_tests.py --test all

Adding another test

You normally do not need to edit run_ascot_tests.py.

Add another object to the tests array in ascot_tests.json:

{
  "name": "my_test",
  "description": "Example custom parameter test.",
  "markers": 5000,
  "birth_energy_eV": 3520000.0,
  "seed": 42,
  "output_prefix": "my_test",
  "overrides": {
    "plasma": {
      "electron_temperature_eV": 15000
    },
    "simulation_options": {
      "ENDCOND_MAX_MILEAGE": 1.0
    }
  }
}

Then run:

python3 run_ascot_tests.py --test my_test

This allows you to perform controlled parameter sweeps such as:

Test A: 1,000 markers
Test B: 10,000 markers
Test C: 100,000 markers

or

Test A: 10 keV plasma
Test B: 15 keV plasma
Test C: 20 keV plasma

or

Test A: 0.5 m mileage limit
Test B: 1.0 m mileage limit
Test C: 2.0 m mileage limit

The same pattern can be used for other values already exposed by the JSON
configuration.

Different simulation workflows

Each test can specify a workflow:

"workflow": "afsi_slowingdown"

uses the AFSI thermal fusion-product source used by the current slowing-down
case.

"workflow": "direct_alpha"

creates direct guiding-center alpha markers without building the AFSI source.
This is useful for lightweight orbit-following and performance tests.

The runner currently provides these two reusable workflows. Adding a completely
different ASCOT5 tutorial/application may require a new workflow function, but
parameter scans within these workflows require only JSON changes.

7. Output structure

Each test is isolated in its own directory:

runs/
├── slowingdown_baseline/
│   ├── ascot.h5
│   ├── input_summary.txt
│   ├── mpi_command.txt
│   ├── slowingdown_baseline_2d_histograms.png
│   ├── slowingdown_baseline_3d_browser.html
│   ├── slowingdown_baseline_summary.json
│   └── slowingdown_baseline_summary.txt
│
├── high_density/
│   ├── ascot.h5
│   └── ...
│
└── test_report.json

This is deliberate: a new test no longer deletes the previous test's
ascot.h5.

Check a result

cat runs/slowingdown_baseline/slowingdown_baseline_summary.txt

Machine-readable result:

cat runs/slowingdown_baseline/slowingdown_baseline_summary.json

Whole-suite report:

cat runs/test_report.json

The 2D histogram is useful for quick comparison, while the Plotly HTML file
can be opened in a browser and rotated interactively.

8. HPC benchmarking workflow

For the SCC / cluster work, the recommended workflow is:

1. Choose parameters in ascot_tests.json
             |
             v
2. Run one or more test cases
             |
             v
3. Keep every test in its own directory
             |
             v
4. Compare elapsed runtime
             |
             v
5. Increase marker count / change physics parameters
             |
             v
6. Repeat with different MPI/OpenMP layouts
             |
             v
7. Use the result summaries and monitoring tools

Useful scaling dimensions include:

marker count

plasma density

plasma temperature

maximum mileage

simulation time limit

MPI process count

OpenMP thread count

number of nodes

For a fair benchmark, keep the physics inputs and random seed fixed while
changing only the HPC parameter under investigation.

9. Important ASCOT5 data note

ASCOT5 stores simulation input and output in HDF5. The project should keep
parameter names and descriptions clear so that later analysis can distinguish
different cases.

The reusable runner follows that principle by:

storing each test in a separate directory

recording the test name and description

writing an input summary

saving the exact MPI command

generating machine-readable JSON results

producing visual output for every test

